# Logseq DB version - what changes vs file-based (markdown) graphs
# The other logseq/* sheets describe FILE-BASED graphs (0.10.x), which is
# what an existing markdown graph is. DB graphs are SQLite-backed and rename
# most of the query surface. Check which kind of graph you opened first.
# https://github.com/logseq/docs/blob/master/db-version-changes.md

## Model
- Blocks and pages are unified as "nodes"; both are referenced with [[...]].
- ((block-ref)) syntax is gone - use [[...]] for blocks too.
- Markdown only; org-mode files are no longer supported.
- `#` and `/` are disallowed in page names.
- Namespaces are no longer baked into the page name (renaming is safe);
  the {{namespace}} macro is deprecated in favour of Library.
- Task markers are gone as keywords - Status, Priority, Deadline and
  Scheduled are properties now. The :LOGBOOK: drawer becomes status history.
- Properties gained real types: numbers and booleans, not just text.

## Attribute renames (advanced queries, :default-queries, config.edn)
:block/content        -> :block/title
:block/original-name  -> :block/title
:block/marker         -> :logseq.property/status
:block/priority       -> :logseq.property/priority
:block/deadline       -> :logseq.property/deadline
:block/scheduled      -> :logseq.property/scheduled

## Removed attributes
:block/journal?    -> tag instead: [?b :block/tags :logseq.class/Journal]
:block/left        -> :block/order
:block/path-refs   -> use the (has-ref ?b ?ref) rule

## Simple-query filter renames
(page-tags ...)        -> (tags ...)
(page-property ...)    -> (property ...)
(has-page-property ...)-> (has-property ...)
(priority A)           -> (priority high)
# (all-page-tags) and (sort-by) are removed.

## Rules that exist in DB graphs
(parent ?p ?c)              recursive block nesting
(has-ref ?b ?r)             block or an ancestor references ?r
(alias ?e2 ?e1)
(class-extends ?p ?c)       class hierarchy
(self-ref ?b ?ref)

## Also note
- Many config.edn keys no longer apply, e.g. :journals-directory,
  :preferred-format, :default-templates, :block-hidden-properties,
  :ref/linked-references-collapsed-threshold, :srs/initial-interval.
- Advanced queries you copied from blogs/forums almost certainly assume
  the file-based attribute names; translate them with the table above.
